"1. Use LensKit version 0.14.4 and implement all data loading, splitting, training, recommendation, and evaluation with APIs compatible with that version.\n2. Load the three raw input files from the working directory exactly as: u.data (MovieLens100K), VideoGames.csv (Amazon Video Games), and user_taggedartists-timestamps.dat (Last.FM).\n3. Parse each dataset into a user\u2013item interaction table with at least user, item, and interaction columns; ignore timestamps except where needed for parsing.\n4. Apply 5-core filtering to every dataset before splitting, iteratively enforcing minimum 5 interactions per user and per item until convergence.\n5. For MovieLens100K and Amazon Video Games, convert ratings > 3 into implicit interactions and discard ratings <= 3.\n6. For Last.FM, treat each observed user\u2013artist event as an implicit interaction; if duplicate user\u2013item events exist, collapse them to one interaction per user\u2013item pair.\n7. After preprocessing, ensure each dataset contains only unique user\u2013item interactions and report basic dataset statistics: number of users, items, and interactions.\n8. Generate exactly 5 distinct random seeds for data splitting and use the same seed set across all datasets and algorithms.\n9. For each dataset and seed, perform a user-based random 80/20 holdout split on interactions, preserving that every test user has at least 1 held-out item and excluding users that cannot support the split if necessary.\n10. Use the same train/test split for all algorithms within a given dataset\u2013seed condition.\n11. Evaluate exactly three algorithms: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop, using standard/default LensKit hyperparameters unless a parameter must be set explicitly for implicit-feedback compatibility.\n12. Configure ALS for implicit-feedback recommendation in a way supported by LensKit 0.14.4; do not use explicit-rating loss on the binarized data.\n13. Train each algorithm separately on each dataset\u2013seed training split and generate top-N recommendations for each eligible test user.\n14. Exclude from recommendation any items already seen in the user\u2019s training interactions.\n15. Compute nDCG@1, nDCG@5, nDCG@10 and Precision@1, Precision@5, Precision@10 for every dataset\u2013algorithm\u2013seed condition.\n16. Use a consistent candidate item set for ranking within each split, based on recommendable items available in the training data and not previously consumed by the user.\n17. Aggregate metrics over users within each dataset\u2013algorithm\u2013seed condition and store both per-user and aggregated results.\n18. Save all experimental outputs to disk in tidy files, including: preprocessed dataset stats, split definitions or seed records, per-user metrics, and aggregated metrics.\n19. Produce a summary table for each dataset comparing the 5-seed mean and variability (at minimum standard deviation) of each metric for ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop.\n20. Conduct a short statistical analysis quantifying split-seed sensitivity, including metric variance across seeds and at least one paired significance test or repeated-measures comparison across algorithms using the 5 matched seeds.\n21. Clearly separate variability due to random splits from differences due to algorithm choice in the reported analysis.\n22. Make the experiment reproducible by fixing and recording all random seeds used for splitting and any model-training randomness.\n23. Include runtime/error handling sufficient to skip or log failed runs without silently corrupting aggregated results.\n24. Verify that each metric is computed on users with non-empty test data and that k values larger than the available recommendation list are handled correctly.\n25. Structure the code so the full experiment can be executed end-to-end from the working directory without manual intervention."